<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kowoj</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1697 CE (approx.) · capital Zacpeten</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya group of the Petén lakes that claimed descent from Mayapan and rivalled the Itza until both fell to Spain in 1697.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to their own traditions, the Kowoj (Couoh) migrated south from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">Mayapan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a> lakes after its fall around 1441, arriving by about 1450. They settled the north-eastern shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peten-itza">Lake Petén Itzá</a> and nearby lakes, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacpeten">Zacpetén</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-salpeten">Lake Salpetén</a> as an important centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kowoj were led by their own lords and at times allied with, and at times fought, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/itza-kingdom-of-nojpeten">Itza kingdom</a> of Nojpetén. Their towns show architecture modelled on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">Mayapan</a>, with temple assemblages and colonnaded halls. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacpeten">Zacpetén</a> revealed ritual deposits and evidence of warfare. Kowoj leaders were involved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itza-people">Itza</a> succession struggles of the 1690s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Spanish conquest of Nojpetén in 1697 ended Kowoj independence. Many fled into the forests, and the survivors were gathered into colonial mission towns around the lake.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1450 CE – 1697 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:4.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1697 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zacpeten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ko'woj, Couoh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1574&amp;focus=kowoj" aria-label="Kowoj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2242 1547 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kowoj lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2562 1803L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2562 1803h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2562 1803h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zacpeten. <a href="/atlas?year=1574&amp;focus=kowoj">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

